Adding custom attributes in Azure AD B2C and retrieving them using Microsoft Graph API involves multiple steps. Below is a step-by-step guide to help you achieve this.


---

Step 1: Register an App in Azure AD B2C

First, you need to register an application that will have the necessary permissions to interact with Microsoft Graph.

1.1. Go to Azure AD B2C Portal

1. Open the Azure Portal: https://portal.azure.com


2. Navigate to Azure AD B2C.



1.2. Register a New Application

1. Under Manage, click App registrations → New registration.


2. Enter a Name (e.g., GraphAPIClient).


3. Select Accounts in any identity provider or organizational directory (for authentication using Azure AD B2C).


4. Click Register.



1.3. Grant API Permissions

1. After registration, go to API permissions → Add a permission.


2. Select Microsoft Graph → Application permissions.


3. Search for and add the following permissions:

User.ReadWrite.All

Directory.ReadWrite.All



4. Click Grant admin consent.



1.4. Create a Client Secret

1. Go to Certificates & secrets → New client secret.


2. Provide a description and expiration time.


3. Click Add, then copy the secret value (you'll need it later).




---

Step 2: Add a Custom Attribute in Azure B2C

Azure AD B2C stores custom attributes as extension properties.

2.1. Create a Custom Attribute

1. In Azure AD B2C, go to User attributes (under Manage).


2. Click Add.


3. Enter an attribute name (e.g., Role).


4. Select String as the data type.


5. Click Create.



After creation, Azure AD B2C automatically prefixes the attribute with extension_ when used in Microsoft Graph.

For example, if you create Role, the actual attribute key will be:

extension_<B2C-EXTENSION-APP-ID>_Role


---

Step 3: Find the Extension App ID

Each B2C tenant has a special B2C Extensions App that manages custom attributes.

3.1. Find the B2C Extension App ID

1. Go to Azure AD B2C → App registrations.


2. Search for an app named b2c-extensions-app.


3. Open the app and copy the Application (client) ID.


4. The extension_<B2C-EXTENSION-APP-ID>_Role format uses this ID.



Example: If the B2C Extension App ID is 1234abcd-5678-efgh-ijkl-9876mnopqrst, the attribute name will be:

extension_1234abcd5678efghijkl9876mnopqrst_Role


---

Step 4: Create a User with a Custom Attribute using Microsoft Graph

Now that we have the custom attribute, we can create a user with it.

4.1. Install Microsoft Graph SDK (if not already installed)

Run the following command in your project:

dotnet add package Microsoft.Graph

4.2. C# Code to Create a User with Custom Attribute

using Microsoft.Graph;
using Microsoft.Identity.Client;
using System;
using System.Net.Http.Headers;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main()
    {
        var clientId = "your-client-id"; // From App Registration
        var clientSecret = "your-client-secret"; // From Client Secret
        var tenantId = "yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com"; // Your B2C Tenant
        var extensionAppId = "your-extension-app-id"; // B2C Extensions App ID

        var scopes = new[] { "https://graph.microsoft.com/.default" };

        IConfidentialClientApplication confidentialClientApp = ConfidentialClientApplicationBuilder
            .Create(clientId)
            .WithClientSecret(clientSecret)
            .WithAuthority($"https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenantId}")
            .Build();

        var authResult = await confidentialClientApp.AcquireTokenForClient(scopes).ExecuteAsync();
        var graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(new DelegateAuthenticationProvider(requestMessage =>
        {
            requestMessage.Headers.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", authResult.AccessToken);
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }));

        var user = new User
        {
            GivenName = "John",
            Surname = "Doe",
            DisplayName = "John Doe",
            MailNickname = "johndoe",
            UserPrincipalName = "johndoe@yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com",
            Mail = "johndoe@example.com",
            PasswordProfile = new PasswordProfile
            {
                ForceChangePasswordNextSignIn = false,
                Password = "P@ssw0rd123"
            },
            PasswordPolicies = "DisablePasswordExpiration",
            Identities = new List<ObjectIdentity>
            {
                new ObjectIdentity
                {
                    SignInType = "emailAddress",
                    Issuer = "yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com",
                    IssuerAssignedId = "johndoe@example.com"
                }
            },
            AdditionalData = new Dictionary<string, object>
            {
                { $"extension_{extensionAppId}_Role", "Admin" } // Assigning Role
            }
        };

        try
        {
            var createdUser = await graphClient.Users
                .Request()
                .AddAsync(user);

            Console.WriteLine($"User created with ID: {createdUser.Id}");
        }
        catch (ServiceException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error creating user: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }
}


---

Step 5: Retrieve the User and Custom Attribute via Microsoft Graph

Once the user is created, you can retrieve their details, including the custom attribute.

5.1. C# Code to Fetch User Details

var userId = "johndoe@yourtenant.onmicrosoft.com"; // UserPrincipalName or Object ID

var user = await graphClient.Users[userId]
    .Request()
    .Select($"id,displayName,mail,extension_{extensionAppId}_Role") // Fetch custom attribute
    .GetAsync();

Console.WriteLine($"User Role: {user.AdditionalData[$"extension_{extensionAppId}_Role"]}");


---

Step 6: Testing the Implementation

1. Run the user creation script to create a user.


2. Run the user retrieval script to verify the Role attribute is stored.




---

Conclusion

Now, you've successfully:

Created a custom attribute in Azure AD B2C.

Stored the role during user creation.

Fetched the role using Microsoft Graph.


This approach allows you to implement role-based access control (RBAC) in Azure AD B2C.

